-- §10b Every `between` as a cross-document REFERENCE, beside whether it lands on a layer here.
# One row per filed reference, with the filing its notation resolves to, or the typed reason it
# resolves to nothing.
#
# Reported, not asserted
#   That is the whole design. A part reference that dangles is a violation and
#   `checks/unresolved_part.sqlc` says so, because a fusion sum cannot be computed without it. A
#   `between` that dangles is ordinary, and the schema says so in the element's own refusals: *a
#   group eliminating against a member that files nothing, or against a layer folded into a
#   different composed layer, is ordinary, and a keyref forcing the subset would make the honest
#   case unfileable*. Writing the part rule's shape here would accuse a filing the model names as
#   correct.
#
# Why nothing pulled it into view
#   This tree's pressure is *a rule reads only what it needs*, so the thing that normally forces a
#   reference into being a named object is the check that reads it. `between` is the one
#   cross-document reference no rule may check, so nothing pulled it into view, and it stayed a
#   raw table read as evidence for a number rather than as a pointer at another document.
#
# A general hole in the pressure model
#   Not a fact about eliminations: anything the model licenses but no rule may judge is invisible
#   to a tree that grows by rules. §7's observations are the answer, and this is one.
-- eliminations/references.sqlc against eliminations/resolved.sqlc, the whole beside the half.
SELECT a.composition                        AS "composition!",
       a.composed_layer                     AS "composed_layer!",
       a.quantity::text                     AS "quantity!",
       a.party                              AS "party!",
       a.layer                              AS "layer!",
       coalesce(r.resolved_filing, '(outside this corpus)') AS "lands_on!",
       (r.resolved_filing IS NOT NULL)      AS "resolves!"
FROM      (
    :compose(eliminations/references.sqlc)
) a
LEFT JOIN (
    :compose(eliminations/resolved.sqlc)
) r USING (composition, composed_layer, quantity, seq)
ORDER BY (r.resolved_filing IS NOT NULL), 1, 2, 3, a.seq
